some of the best-known characters in the  comics universe  Captain America The Incredible Hulk the  Mighty Thor Iron Man even spider-man  were co-created by the king of comics  Jack Kirby but Kirby never owned the  copyright to the characters he drew his  employer does that's marvel many of  Kirby's most famous characters debuted  between 1958 and 1963 when artists had  little bargaining power but then  Congress passed a law in 1976 allowing  creators to reclaim the rights to their  work after a period of time Kirby died  in 1994 but now his family is suing to  get the copyright to his Marvel  characters under Congress's 1976 law  what's at stake money potentially a lot  of it Disney acquired Marvel in 2009 for  four billion dollars and in the deal got  the rights to all those Kirby characters  this led to movies like The Avengers and  sequels to Iron Man Thor Captain America  collectively these movies made more than  four billion more dollars at the box  office alone two lower courts have  already sided with Marvel the Kirby  estate has asked the Supreme Court to  hear their case it hinges on something  called a work made for hire the  copyright in a work belongs to the  author who created it however if a work  is made for hire the employer in this  case Marvel is considered the author not  the employee who created the work Jack  Kirby the Kirby's contend that they have  the right to terminate Marvel's  copyright because of Congress's 1976 law  the court should decide whether or not  to take the case in September if the  court takes the case and rules in the  Kirby's favor the family could be  entitled to some of those superhero  Billy's
 

